course: biology
lesson: 51
title: How animals hide: camouflage and mimicry
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Out in the wild, being seen can mean being eaten, and being seen by your dinner means going hungry, so many animals have become masters of hide and seek.
explain: Camouflage means looking like the things around you: a stick insect looks like a twig, a polar bear looks like snow, a flounder looks like the sandy seabed, a tawny owl looks like bark.
explain: Mimicry is a different trick: a harmless animal copies a dangerous one, like the hoverfly dressed as a wasp, or copies something nobody wants to eat, like the caterpillar dressed as a bird dropping.
explain: Think of the class stick insect that nobody can find for five minutes: it is right there in the tank, but your eyes hunt for an insect shape and see only twigs.
explain: Cartoons show a chameleon turning tartan on a tartan blanket and vanishing; the real chameleon cannot copy patterns at all and changes colour mostly to show its mood and to warm up or cool down.
fact: A chameleon changes colour by moving tiny crystals in special skin cells, which changes how light bounces off its skin; it does this mostly to signal to other chameleons and to control its temperature.
fact: Stick insects are long, thin and brown or green, and they hold still or sway gently like a twig in the wind; one from China measured about 64 cm with its legs stretched out.
fact: The arctic fox grows a thick white coat for the snowy winter and a thinner brown-grey coat for summer, so it matches the ground in both seasons.
fact: A polar bear's hairs are see-through and hollow and its skin is black; the clear hairs scatter light, so the bear looks white against the ice; zoo polar bears have even turned a bit green when tiny algae grew inside their hollow hairs.
fact: A flounder lies flat on the seabed, flicks sand over its edges and changes its pattern to match; a baby flounder swims upright, then one eye moves over to the other side of its head.
fact: An octopus has thousands of colour cells and can raise bumps on its skin to copy rocks and weed in under a second, even though scientists think it cannot see colour the way we do.
fact: A hoverfly is a harmless fly with wasp-like yellow and black stripes; birds that have learned stripes mean a sting leave it alone, and this trick is called mimicry.
fact: A hoverfly has one pair of wings and can hover still in the air, while a wasp has two pairs.
fact: Every zebra has its own stripe pattern; scientists think the main job of stripes is to keep biting flies away, which they tested by putting striped and plain coats on horses and counting fly landings, and they may also confuse lions in a running herd.
fact: In smoky 1800s England, soot blackened tree trunks, so dark peppered moths hid better, birds ate the pale ones they could see, and dark moths became common; when the air got cleaner the pale ones came back.
fact: A tawny owl sleeps by day in streaky brown feathers that match bark; if small birds or crows find it, they mob it with loud alarm calls.
fact: Young swallowtail caterpillars look like shiny bird droppings, and older ones, too big to pass as a dropping, turn green with fake eye spots like a little snake.
fact: Leaf insects are flat and green with leaf veins and even brown spots like bite marks, and some rock gently like a leaf in the breeze.
exercise: What does camouflage mean? | Looking like the things around you so you are hard to see | Copying the sound of a much bigger, scarier animal to frighten enemies | Changing your shape to run faster | Hiding in a burrow all day
exercise: Why does a chameleon at the zoo turn darker when another chameleon walks up? | It is showing its mood to the other chameleon | It is copying the colour of the other chameleon | It is getting ready to fall asleep for the night | Its skin always goes dark whenever it is hungry
exercise: How does a chameleon's skin change colour? | Tiny crystals in skin cells move and change how light bounces off | It squeezes coloured juice out of its mouth and spreads it over its skin | It swaps its skin for a new one each time | It soaks up colour from whatever it touches
exercise: Why does a stick insect sway gently on its twig? | So it looks like a twig moving in the wind | Because its legs are too weak to hold still | To shake off the ants that climb up its legs | To warm itself up in the bright morning sunshine
exercise: Why is an arctic fox white in winter and brown in summer? | So it matches the snow in winter and the rocks and plants in summer | Because the cold winter sun slowly bleaches its thick brown fur to white | Because it eats only white food in winter | Because old foxes are white and young ones brown
exercise: What colour is the skin under a polar bear's fur? | Black | White | Pink | Pale yellow
exercise: How does a flounder hide on the seabed? | It lies flat, flicks sand over itself and matches the bottom | It digs a deep tunnel and pulls a stone over the hole | It sucks in water and puffs itself up like a round rock | It swims upright among seaweed like a stick
exercise: What can an octopus do in less than a second to look like a rock? | Change colour and raise little bumps on its skin | Grow a hard grey shell all over its soft body | Pull its arms inside its head | Breathe out a thick cloud of sand and hide in it
exercise: Why do birds leave a stripy hoverfly alone? | They have learned that yellow and black stripes mean a sting | The hoverfly tastes of mint and birds hate the taste of mint | The hoverfly is too fast for any bird | Birds cannot see yellow at all
exercise: What do scientists think is the main job of a zebra's stripes? | Keeping biting flies away | Keeping the zebra cool by catching the wind | Hiding the zebra in tall green grass | Showing how old the zebra is
exercise: Why did dark peppered moths become common in smoky 1800s cities? | Soot made trunks dark, so birds ate the pale moths they could see | Smoke turned the pale moths dark, the way the sun gives people a tan | Dark moths liked the taste of soot | The pale moths flew away to the seaside
exercise: How does a tawny owl stay hidden in the daytime? | Its streaky brown feathers match the bark and it sits very still | It flies high above the clouds all day long, where nobody can see it | It hides under water in the pond | It turns white when it sleeps
exercise: Why does a young swallowtail caterpillar look like a bird dropping? | Birds do not eat bird droppings, so it is safe in the open | It wants to scare away other caterpillars from its leaf | Bird droppings are the caterpillar's favourite food, so it copies them | It is ill and will soon change back
exercise: What has a leaf insect got on its body to make the disguise better? | Veins and brown spots like a chewed leaf | Bright red warning stripes down its back | A fluffy coat of white hair | Spines like a cactus all over it
exercise: What is the difference between camouflage and mimicry? | Camouflage blends in; mimicry copies something predators avoid | They are two names for exactly the same trick, used by the same animals | Camouflage is for plants and mimicry is for animals | Mimicry only happens at night
exercise: What happens to a baby flounder's eyes as it grows? | One eye moves over to the other side of its head | Both eyes close up forever | It grows a third eye on top | Its eyes slowly move down to its belly to look at the sand
test: my cartoon chameleon turned tartan on a tartan rug and vanished. real? | No, they can't copy patterns; colour changes mostly for mood and warmth | Yes, they copy any pattern they sit on | Yes, but only red and green tartan, not the blue sort | No, chameleons never change colour at all
test: Mate says that stripy thing on the flower is a wasp, run! It hovers dead still and has one pair of wings. What is it? | A harmless hoverfly copying a wasp | A wasp with two of its wings tucked away | A baby wasp that has not grown its sting | A bumblebee that has lost all of its fur
test: If polar bear fur is not really white, why does it look white to us? | The clear hollow hairs scatter the light | The black skin shines through it as white | The bear rolls in fresh snow every morning | The ice reflects off it just like a mirror
test: What would happen to a white arctic fox if the snow melted early in spring? | It would stand out on brown ground until its summer coat grew | It would turn brown overnight, as soon as it saw the ground | It would be safer than ever | It would dig into the ice to hide
test: A zoo once found its polar bears had turned a bit green. What had happened? | Tiny algae had grown inside their hollow hairs | They had eaten too much spinach | Their skin had turned green with cold | The keepers had painted them green for a summer show
test: How could scientists test whether stripes really keep flies away? | Put striped coats and plain coats on horses and count fly landings | Paint all the zebras in one herd plain brown and see if they get angry | Ask lions whether stripes confuse them | Count the stripes on every zebra
test: What would happen to the peppered moths if a town became very smoky again? | Dark moths would hide better and become more common again | Pale moths would grow soot on their wings | All moths would leave the town forever | Nothing, moths do not care at all what colour the tree trunks are
test: ugh crows screaming their heads off at that tree, what's their problem | They've probably found a sleeping owl and are mobbing it | They're building a nest together | They're warning each other about the rain coming in | They're fighting over a worm
test: Which is true about the octopus? | It matches colours well, though it may not see colour like we do | It can only change colour when it sees something red | It hides by growing a shell like a snail | It changes colour only when it sleeps
test: Why might the swallowtail caterpillar change its disguise when it grows bigger? | A big bird dropping would look odd, so a fake snake face works better | It gets bored of the old colour | Big caterpillars no longer have enemies, so they don't need to hide | It must match the flower it will eat next
test: If you designed a hiding insect for a desert, what would it look like? | Sandy coloured, flat and still like a pebble or dry twig | Bright green with shiny wings | Black with white spots | Bright red all over, to warn every animal in the desert away
exam: Identify the principal purpose for which the chameleon is understood to alter its colouration. | Social signalling and the regulation of body temperature | The exact reproduction of patterns upon which it rests, for concealment | The attraction of insects towards its tongue | The expulsion of surplus water from the skin
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pelage of the polar bear? | The hairs are clear and hollow, and the skin beneath is black | The hairs are white and the underlying skin is white | The hairs are solid and the skin is pink | The hairs are black and appear white by reflection of snow
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the hoverfly. | It is a harmless fly whose resemblance to a wasp deters birds | It possesses a sting considerably more potent than that of the wasp | It bears two complete pairs of wings in the manner of the true wasp | It is avoided by birds on account of its exceedingly disagreeable odour
exam: It is given that the pale form of the peppered moth declined in industrial towns. Ascertain the reason. | Soot-darkened bark rendered pale moths conspicuous to birds | Industrial smoke dyed the wings of the pale moths a darker shade | Pale moths migrated to rural districts | Dark moths consumed the pale moths
